+/-!
+
+# Racah's closed formula with a vanishing spin
+
+`racah6j` with `j₁ = 0` or `j₃ = 0` is `(-1)^(a + b + c - K) / √((2j₁₂ + 1)(2j₂₃ + 1))`.
+
+## i. Overview
+
+All spins are doubled naturals. For `{0 b b; c f f}` and `{a b e; 0 e b}` (with `f = b + c - 2K`,
+`e = a + b - 2K`, `K` at most the relevant spins) Racah's sum has a single term.
+
+1. The arguments of `racahSum6` are `t - m` for four naturals `m`, one of them `0`, and `t + n`
+   for three naturals `n`, one of them `0`; then only the term `t` is nonzero
+   (`racahSum6_single`).
+2. Write `b = K + r`, `c = K + q` (respectively `a = K + q`, `b = K + r`). Then `t = K + q + r`
+   and the single term is `(-1)^t (t + 1)! / (q! K! r!)`. Of the four triangle coefficients, two
+   are `1 / (n + 1)` and two are `Δ² = K! q! r! / (K + q + r + 1)!`, which cancels the term
+   (`triCoeff_mul_edge`).
+3. This gives `racah6j_zero_left` and `racah6j_zero_right`.
+
+## ii. Key results
+
+- `racahSum6_single`: Racah's sum when only one term survives.
+- `racah6j_zero_left`: `racah6j 0 b b c f f` with `f = b + c - 2K`.
+- `racah6j_zero_right`: `racah6j a b e 0 e b` with `e = a + b - 2K`.
+
+## iii. Table of contents
+
+- A. Racah's sum with a single term
+- B. Racah's formula with a vanishing spin
+
+## iv. References
+
+- G. Racah, *Theory of complex spectra. II*, Phys. Rev. 62, 438 (1942).
+- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §34.4, https://dlmf.nist.gov/34.4.
+
+-/
